ion schema:\n- candidateId: \"candidate:e9fa0614-0b95-4643-93bb-85b1947706ba\"\n- reason: \"This is a reprint of the same TypeScript incremental build optimization content already represented by candidate f3812107; identical title and semantic similarity, so admitting it would duplicate existing pool content.\"\n- decision: \"reject\"\n- relevance: \"direct\" (it is relevant to the interest, but duplicate)\n- matchedInterestIds: [\"evaluation:interest:typescript\"]\n- contentValue: \"substantive\" (the content is substantive but duplicate) — actually for a reject, contentValue can be substantive or low_value. The content itself is substantive but it's a duplicate.\n- novelty: \"semantic_duplicate\"\n- temporalValidity: \"valid\" (not stale per info)\n- negativeConstraint: \"clear\"\n- interestRevisions: [{\"interestId\":\"evaluation:interest:typescript\",\"revision\":1}]\n- preferenceRevisions: [{\"scopeKey\":\"interest:evaluation:interest:typescript\",\"revision\":0}] — the preference revision for the interest scope. Exploration preference: scopeKey \"exploration\", revision 0. Should I include it? The schema asks for preferenceRevisions array. Since no directions changed, I should include the current revisions? Hmm. Let me include the interest preference revision (0) as it exists in the material.\n\nActually let me think about what preferenceRevisions means: \"Submit the exact Interest and Preference revisions from Context\". The interest preference scope is \"interest:evaluation:interest:typescript\" revision 0, and exploration preference scope \"exploration\" revision 0. So I include those.\n\n- preferenceAlignment: [] (no directions exist)\n- reasonCode: \"semantic_duplicate\"\n- duplicateOfCandidateId: \"candidate:f3812107-b8f1-4e75-bdbc-f161015347df\"\n\nThen after rejecting this, the pool still has available: 1, and totalShortfall: 5 (target 6). We need to search more to fill the gap.\n\nLet me first commit the rejection for the pending batch, then continue searching.\n\nActually wait — should I read the source candidate first to confirm the duplicate? The description explicitly says \"同一项增量构建优化的转载\" (reprint of the same incremental build optimization). The potential duplicate has the same title and semantic similarity. Evidence is quite clear. Reading would only confirm; but the candidate description is self-declaring a reprint. I think it's safe to reject without reading.\n\nLet me commit the rejection and simultaneously... no, the instructions say: \"If no admission batch is waiting, or a committed batch leaves a real gap, plan searches\". I can commit the rejection first, then search. Actually I can do the commit and searches in parallel? The searches don't depend on the commit result, but it's cleaner to commit first. However, since the rejection decision is already determined, I can run the commit along with new searches in the same block since they're independent. Let me do that to be efficient.\n\nActually, let me be careful: the commit's outcome doesn't affect the search planning. The gap remains regardless. So I can run searches in parallel with the commit.\n\nLet me plan searches. Interest: \"TypeScript 编译器演进\" (TypeScript compiler evolution). Sources: open_web only.\n\nPrevious queries already tried:\n- \"TypeScript 编译器性能更新\" → 1 candidate (the duplicate)\n- \"TypeScript 编译器演进\" → 1 candidate (the duplicate, merged)\n- \"TypeScript compiler evolution roadmap\" → merged duplicate\n- \"TypeScript compiler performance improvements\" → merged duplicate\n\nSo all these searches converge on the same candidate. The source seems to have limited distinct results (test environment). Let me try other query angles to find new content:\n- \"TypeScript 新版本 编译器\"\n- \"TypeScript compiler new features\"\n- \"TypeScript 5.0 编译器\"\n- \"TypeScript compiler internals\"\n- \"TypeScript 编译器 架构\"\n\nLet me try a few more varied queries to find non-duplicate content.\n\nLet me commit the rejection and run searches in parallel.\n\n